  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows the cutoff low over\nIndiana has opened up and is drifting east. The Four Corners \nridge is amplifying ahead of a deepening trof over the PACNW. \n\nThe enjoyable weather of the Corn Belt continues with temps \ncooler than typical early August readings. Winds, hard to ignore\nSaturday, are blowing at 0-5 knots out of the south as return \nflow develops. Skies are mostly cloud-less, but wildfire smoke \ndrifting in from the north is blocking about 1/4 of of our \nsunshine this afternoon. Very little smoke is found at the \nsurface, it's generally about 5k to 8k feet off the sfc.\n\nThe patchy dense fog found this morning is not expected to \nrepeat overnight, at least not in Nebraska. Wind speeds of 5-15 \nknots and dewpoint depressions of about two degrees should be \nenough to keep it at bay. Higher max RH numbers in western Iowa\nwill make fog development more of an uncertainty there. It's \nnot currently forecast.",
